\image(figures/wide_resnet_training.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Three complete WRN-16 fits on 16x16 average-pooled images share fixed subsets and augmentation seeds. Width and branch dropout vary; validation selects each paired checkpoint before its test evaluation.)
\alttext(Training and validation curves for width one, width two and width two with dropout .3.)

The selected checkpoints show substantial overfitting and do not support a general
width/dropout ranking:

| Width k | Dropout p | Selected epoch | Validation NLL | Test NLL | Test accuracy |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 0 | 7 | 2.218949 | 2.200018 | 0.312500 |
| 2 | 0 | 1 | 2.200805 | 2.247619 | 0.187500 |
| 2 | .3 | 1 | 2.265416 | 2.207598 | 0.187500 |

Only the narrow network improves on its first-epoch validation loss within 8 epochs; both
width-two runs select epoch 1, so on 64 test images they differ by noise rather than by
dropout. The training
curves use augmented images and batch BN moments; the restored inference training
losses printed above use saved moving moments and need not match those curves.

> Native confusion matrices

These matrices use the held-out predictions from the executed program above.
The native `ConfusionMatrix` SVG API labels exact counts with true classes
running from top to bottom and predicted classes from left to right.

